.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SW_VERS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sw_vers
.Nd display operating system version information
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -productName | -productVersion | -productVersionExtra | -buildVersion
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility displays identification and version information for the
currently running operating system.

The primary source of operating system information is the
.Pa /etc/os-release
file.
If this file is unavailable, the alternative location
.Pa /usr/lib/os-release
is used.
The
.Sy NAME
and
.Sy VERSION
fields provide the operating system name and version,
respectively.

If either field is unavailable or contains invalid information,
the corresponding property is obtained through
.Xr uname 2 ,
using the
.Sy sysname
and
.Sy release
fields, respectively.

When invoked without options,
.Nm
prints all available version properties:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sw_vers
ProductName:              Ubuntu
ProductVersion:           26.10
ProductVersionExtra:      (a)
BuildVersion:             26A100
.Ed
.Pp
The following properties are reported:
.Bl -tag -width ProductVersionExtra
.It Sy ProductName
The operating system or distribution name.
.It Sy ProductVersion
The version identifier associated with the current operating system
release.
.It Sy ProductVersionExtra
An additional version identifier.
Its original purpose in macOS is to identify the Rapid Security
Response version installed on the operating system, when applicable.
For compatibility with macOS, this implementation always returns
.Sy (a) .
.It Sy BuildVersion
The build identifier assigned to the installed operating system.
.El
.Pp
Each property is printed on a separate line using the format
.Sy PropertyName: Value .
When an individual property is selected, only its corresponding
value is printed.
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options select an individual property to display.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -productName
Display the operating system name.
.It Fl -productVersion
Display the operating system version.
.It Fl -productVersionExtra
Display the additional version identifier.
This option does not support the single-dash compatibility form.
.It Fl -buildVersion
Display the system build identifier.
.El
.Pp
All option names are case-insensitive.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Display the operating system name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sw_vers --productName
Ubuntu
.Ed
.Pp
Display the operating system version:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sw_vers --productVersion
26.10
.Ed
.Pp
Display the additional version identifier:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sw_vers --productVersionExtra
(a)
.Ed
.Pp
Display the build identifier:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sw_vers --buildVersion
26A100
.Ed
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
This implementation aims to provide compatibility with the
.Nm
utility distributed with macOS, including its output properties
and command-line interface.

For compatibility with older invocations, options may also be
specified using a single leading dash.
This convention does not apply to
.Fl -productVersionExtra ,
which was introduced in a later version of macOS and does not
provide a single-dash equivalent.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/os-release
Primary source of operating system identification and version data.
.It Pa /usr/lib/os-release
Alternative source of operating system identification and version data.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility originally appeared in Mac OS X 10.0 Cheetah.
Its behavior was subsequently modified in macOS Big Sur and later
releases.

The
.Sy ProductVersionExtra
property was introduced in macOS Big Sur.
Originally, it identified the Rapid Security Response version
installed on the operating system, when available.

This implementation is part of SysUtils and aims to reproduce the
behavior and interface of the macOS utility while adapting its
information sources and execution environment to other operating
systems.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr uname 1 ,
.Xr machine 1 ,
.Xr uname 2
